[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Software engineering focuses on building software products and covers all the activities involved in developing a software system from initial idea to final product, using general-purpose design principles and patterns.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In contrast, software engineering focuses on building software products.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "as Table 14.6 shows, a software engineering program borrows from both computer science and engineering curricula.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "It fits into computer science as a broader, more applied branch that uses computing as a tool to address general problems, rather than focusing solely on hardware design or abstract theory.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "software engineering is more general, is more applied, and has a broader scope than computer science.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "We can view computing in a similar light. We can concentrate on the computers and programming languages, or we can view them as tools to be used in designing and implementing a solution to a problem.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Technical quality is measured by operational metrics such as uptime, maintenance costs, and modification costs, i.e., quality assessed through how the software performs within cost parameters and how the vendor delivers cost-effective performance.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "We measure the quality [of our software] by the obvious metrics: up versus down time, maintenance costs, costs connected with modifications, and the like. In other words, we manage development based on operational performance within cost parameters.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Business value, on the other hand, is about the broader impact in the business context—whether the project delivers clear business value (e.g., quantified outcomes like number of claims processed) and aligns with purposeful objectives—so ROI and decisions across organizations can be meaningfully compared.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "If there is not clear (expected) business value  (expressed quantitatively: number  of  claims  processed, etc.)  then  we  can't launch a systems project.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The difference in views is disturbing, because it means that calculations of ROI cannot be compared across organizations.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Why each is important: technical quality matters because it directly affects the system’s reliability and cost of operation, guiding development decisions to deliver dependable performance within budget; business value matters because it links quality to what the customer or organization actually gains, guiding whether the effort is worth pursuing and enabling cross-organization ROI comparisons.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "We measure the quality [of our software] by the obvious metrics: up versus down time, maintenance costs, costs connected with modifications, and the like. In other words, we manage development based on operational performance within cost parameters.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The difference in views is disturbing, because it means that calculations of ROI cannot be compared across organizations.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "... one [on] which we focus a great deal of attention. I guess I am surprised to learn that companies would contract with companies for their technical value, at the relative expense of business value. If anything, we err on the other side!",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The issue of business versus technical value is near and dear to our heart ... and one [on] which we focus a great deal of attention.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Additionally, quality is contextual: users judge software to be high quality if it does what they want in a way that is easy to learn and easy to use, showing how quality can be perceived from both product and user perspectives within the business context.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Users judge software to be of high quality if it does what they want in a way that is easy to learn and easy to use. However, sometimes quality and functionality are intertwined; if something is hard to learn or use but its functionality is worth the trouble, then it is still considered to have high quality.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

