[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "To describe the morning readiness process, use Lai notation to model it as a static process where inputs (e.g., alarm time, available clothing, necessary items) are transformed into outputs (you are ready to leave). Lai notation emphasizes that a static model depicts the process and that, in the Lai approach, the left-hand column of a condition box lists artifacts and the right-hand column is the artifact state.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Lai's approach can be applied to modeling software development processes; later in this chapter, we use it to model the risk involved in development.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Other templates define relations, process states, operations, analysis, actions, and roles. Graphical diagrams represent the relationships between elements, capturing the main relationships and secondary ones. For example, Figure 2.11 illustrates the process of starting a car. The 'initiate' box represents the entrance conditions, and the 'park' box represents an exit condition.The left-hand column of a condition box lists artifacts, and the right-hand column is the artifact state.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Three software development life-cycle models mentioned in the provided context are: 1) Waterfall model, 2) Prototyping-based design approach, and 3) Reuse-based design approach. Each model has typical activities, inputs, and outputs discussed in the text.",
    "citations": [],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet_intro",
    "segment": "Waterfall model",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The waterfall model has been used to prescribe software development activities in a variety of contexts. For example, it was the basis for software development deliverables in U.S. Department of Defense contracts for many years, defined in Department of Defense Standard 2167-A. Associated with each process activity were milestones and deliverables, so that project managers could use the model to gauge how close the project was to completion at a given point in time.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "For instance, 'unit and integration testing' in the waterfall ends with the milestone 'code modules written, tested, and integrated'; the intermediate deliverable is a copy of the tested code. Next, the code can be turned over to the system testers so it can be merged with other system components (hardware or software) and tested as a larger whole.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The waterfall model can be very useful in helping developers lay out what they need to do. Its simplicity makes it easy to explain to customers who are not familiar with software development; it makes explicit which intermediate products are necessary  in  order  to  begin  the  next  stage  of  development.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Prototyping-based design",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "design can involve a prototyping process,where many of the design decisions are explored so that developers can choose an appropriate approach",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Reuse-based design",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "a reuse process, where previously generated design components are included in the current design.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "General note on inputs/outputs across models: Every software development process model includes system requirements as input and a delivered product as output.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Every  software  development  process  model  includes  system  requirements  as input and a delivered product as output.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Static modeling uses Lai notation to model a process at any level of detail, focusing on the relationships among the roles, activities, and artifacts, and how artifact state is tracked over time.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Lai's approach can be applied to modeling software development processes; later in  this  chapter, we  use  it  to  model  the  risk  involved  in  development. However, to demonstrate its use and its ability to capture many facets of a complex activity, we apply it to a relatively simple but familiar process, driving an automobile. Table 2.1 contains a description of the key resource in this process, a car.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "There are many ways to model a process statically. In the early 1990s, Lai (1991) developed a comprehensive process notation that is intended to enable someone to model any process at any level of detail. It builds on a paradigm where people perform roles while resources perform activities, leading to the production of artifacts. The process model shows the relationships among the roles, activities, and artifacts, and state tables show information about the completeness of each artifact at a given time.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In particular, the elements of a process are viewed in terms of seven types:",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Dynamic modeling enables enacting and simulating a process so we can watch how resources and artifacts flow as activities occur and compare outcomes under different choices.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A desirable property of a process model is the ability to enact the process, so that we can watch what happens to resources and artifacts as activities occur. In other words, we want to describe a model of the process and then watch as software shows us how resources flow through activities to become outputs.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Dynamic modeling supports testing and decision-making, for example by simulating how many testers are needed or when to initiate testing to finish on schedule, and by adding or omitting activities to see their effects.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "For example, we can use a dynamic process model to help us decide how many testers we need or when we must initiate testing in order to finish on schedule. Similarly, we can include or exclude activities to see their effects on effort and schedule.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Facilitates human understanding and communication. The technique should be able to represent the process in a form that most customers and developers can understand, encouraging communication about the process and agreement on its form and improvements.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Facilitates  human understanding and communication. The technique should be able to represent the process in a form that most customers and developers can understand, encouraging communication about the process and agreement on its form and improvements. The technique should include sufficient information to allow one or more people to actually perform the process.And the model or tool should form a basis for training.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Supports process improvement. The technique should identify the essential components of a development or maintenance process. It should allow reuse of processes or  subprocesses  on  subsequent  projects, compare  alternatives, and  estimate  the impact of changes before the process is actually put into practice. Similarly, the technique should assist in selecting tools and techniques for the process, in encouraging organizational learning, and in supporting continuing evolution of the process.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Supports process improvement. The technique should identify the essential components of a development or maintenance process. It should allow reuse of processes or  subprocesses  on  subsequent  projects, compare  alternatives, and  estimate  the impact of changes before the process is actually put into practice. Similarly, the technique should assist in selecting tools and techniques for the process, in encouraging organizational learning, and in supporting continuing evolution of the process.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Supports process management. The technique should allow the process to be project-specific. Then, developers and customers should be able to reason about attributes of software creation or evolution. The technique should also support planning and forecasting, monitoring and managing the process, and measuring key process characteristics.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Supports  process  management. The  technique  should  allow  the  process  to  be project-specific. Then, developers and customers should be able to reason about attributes of software creation or evolution. The technique should also support planning and forecasting, monitoring and managing the process, and measuring key process characteristics.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Provides automated guidance in performing the process. The technique should define all or part of the software development environment, provide guidance and suggestions, and retain reusable process representations for later use.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Provides automated guidance in performing the process. The technique should define all or part of the software development environment, provide guidance and suggestions, and retain reusable process representations for later use.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Supports automated process execution. The technique should automate all or part of  the  process, support  cooperative work, capture relevant measurement data, and enforce rules to ensure process integrity.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Supports automated process execution. The technique should automate all or part of  the  process, support  cooperative work, capture relevant measurement data, and enforce rules to ensure process integrity.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "bullet_intro",
        "segment": "Prescriptive vs. descriptive process models:",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Many process models are described in the software engineering literature. Some are prescriptions for  the  way  software  development  should  progress, and  others  are descriptions of the way software development is done in actuality.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Prescriptive process models specify how software development should progress and are used to guide behavior and enforce a defined way of working.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Some are prescriptions for  the  way  software  development  should  progress",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A process model is useful for guiding your behavior when you are working with a group.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Descriptive process models describe how software development is actually done, capturing current practices to understand and improve them.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "and  others  are descriptions of the way software development is done in actuality.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "When a group writes down a description of its development process, it forms a common understanding of the activities, resources, and constraints involved in software development.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

