[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "A good source for detailed treatment of design patterns is Gamma et al. (1995), which is described as a good place to start for learning how to build software design from a collection of patterns.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The collection presented by Gamma et al. (1995) is a good place to start.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Design patterns codify design decisions and best practices for solving a particular design problem; they are templates for a solution that must be modified and adapted for each use, and they aim to improve a design's modularity.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A design pattern codifies design decisions and best practices for solving a particular design problem according to design principles.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The main goal of design patterns is to improve a design's modularity.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Design patterns are not the same as software libraries; they are not packaged solutions that can be used as is, but templates to be adapted for each context, similar to how elementary data structures must be instantiated and specialized.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Design patterns are not the same as software libraries; they are not packaged solutions that can be used as is. Rather, they are templates for a solution that must be modified and adapted for each particular use.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "The patterns add abstraction and indirection to improve modularity, but they can seem overly complicated and introduce more classes, associations, and method calls, trading some simplicity for flexibility.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The patterns add extra classes, associations, and method calls, they sometimes can seem overly complicated.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "This complexity improves modularity, even at the expense of other quality attributes such as performance or ease of development.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "In sum, design patterns are a collection of proven solutions that improve modularity and provide reusable design guidance, but they require careful application to avoid unnecessary complexity and cost.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Let us look at some of the popular patterns in more detail.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The main goal of design patterns is to improve a design's modularity.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "This complexity improves modularity, even at the expense of other quality attributes such as performance or ease of development.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "The Therac-25 accidents illustrate that most computer-related accidents result from requirements faults, not from coding faults, and that the underlying cause of the problems was a timing error that was difficult to reproduce. To design safer systems, Leveson and Turner advocate fundamental software engineering practices: documentation should be done as development progresses, not afterward; software quality assurance practices should be an integral part of the development process with standards set early and used to evaluate intermediate products; and simple designs are easier to understand, code, and test than complex ones.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "most computer-related accidents result from requirements faults, not from coding faults",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The underlying cause of the problems was a timing error that was difficult to reproduce.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Documentation should be done as development progresses, not afterward.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Software quality assurance practices should be an integral part of the development process. These practices should include standards that are set early and used to evaluate intermediate products.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Simple designs are easier to understand, code, and test than complex ones.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Encapsulation supports reuse by isolating implementation details behind interfaces, allowing composite objects to manipulate attributes only via their advertised interfaces and letting components be swapped as long as the replacement adheres to the same interface.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "An advantage of object composition is its support of modularity; the composite class knows nothing about the implementations of its object-based attributes and can manipulate these attributes only by using their interfaces.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "as long as the two blocks are the same size and shape.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The only access that other objects have to a given object's data is via a set of access functions that the object advertises in its interface.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Information hiding further supports reuse by concealing data representations and other design decisions behind an object's interface, so the underlying implementation can change without perturbing the rest of the system.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In OO design, we decompose a system into objects and their abstract types. That is, each object (module) is an instance of an abstract data type. In this sense, each object hides its data representation from other objects.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "the only access that other objects have to a given object's data is via a set of access functions that the object advertises in its interface.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Criteria for deciding whether to reuse a class in a new system include the reuse-focused perspectives described for OO today: reuse approaches are compositional or generative, components should be classified and catalogued, and a retrieval system helps select components; OO encourages building designs around invariant components that can be applied to multiple systems.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Approaches to reuse are either compositional or generative.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The components must be classified or catalogued, and a retrieval system must be used to scan and select components for use in a new system.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Because OOD encourages software engineers to build a design around invariant components, many parts of the design and code can be applied to multiple systems.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Separating system-wide design from program-level design helps manage work more effectively: it is convenient to separate design tasks that require consideration of the entire system from design tasks that pertain to individual software units, because the latter can be partitioned into distinct work assignments and assigned to separate design teams. This also allows the architecture-design phase to focus on identifying the major software units and their interfaces, so we can parallelize the rest of the design work. Additionally, the system design and its approval serve as a prerequisite before discussing and generating designs of the individual programs.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "for the purposes of project management, it is convenient to separate design tasks that require consideration of the entire system from design tasks that pertain to individual software units, because the latter can be partitioned into distinct work assignments and assigned to separate design teams.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Thus, the more we can restrict ourselves during the architecture-design phase to identifying the major software units and their interfaces, the more we can parallelize the rest of the design work.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The design is then reviewed by the customer.When approved, the overall system design is used to generate the designs of the individual programs involved.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

