[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Strategy for finding a reusable component involves building an inventory and using a feature-based matrix to identify components that are needed across multiple projects.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The Council created an inventory of components and formed a matrix with the features of all past  and  planned  projects.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Then, each feature was rated in terms  of  whether  it  had  been implemented and was still needed, had been implemented but was no longer needed, or had not been implemented but was still needed.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Those features that were needed and were common to more than one project were targeted for reuse.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In fact, some were redesigned to make them more reusable.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet_intro",
    "segment": "Guidelines for consumers selecting a component for reuse",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Does the component perform the function or provide the data you need?",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "If minor modification is required, is it less modification than building the component from scratch?",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Is the component well-documented, so you can understand it without having to verify its implementation line by line?",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Is there a complete record of the component's test and revision history, so you can be certain that it contains no faults?",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Guidelines for producers of reusable components",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Make your component general, using parameters and anticipating similar conditions to the ones in which your system will invoke your components.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Separate dependencies so that sections likely to need change are isolated from those that are likely to remain the same.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Keep the component interface general and well-defined.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Include information about any faults found and fixed.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Use clear naming conventions.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Document the data structures and algorithms.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Keep the communication and error-handling sections separate and easy to modify.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Approaches and infrastructure for reuse",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Approaches to reuse are either compositional or generative.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "For this kind of reuse, the components are stored in a library (often called the reuse repository ).",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The components must be classified or catalogued, and a retrieval system must be used to scan and select components for use in a new system.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Classification and organization of reusable components",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "One of the biggest obstacles to reuse is the need to search through a large set of software products to find the best one for a particular need.The job is akin to sifting through a junkyard of books rather than visiting a library.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The solution is component classification , where collections of reusable components are organized and catalogued according to a classification scheme.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "The most critical standard is the need for a direct correspondence between the program design components and the program code components.",
        "citations": [
