[
    {
        "role": "bullet_intro",
        "segment": "Coding standards (for a language of your choice)",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Once your code is complete, others are likely to use it in a variety of ways. For example, as we shall see in later chapters, a separate team may test the code. Or another set of people may integrate your software with other programs to build and test subsystems and finally the whole system. Even after the system is up and running, changes may be needed, either because of a fault or because the customer wants to change the way the system performs its functions.You may not be part of those maintenance or test teams, so it is essential that you organize, format, and document your code to make it easy for others to understand what it does and how it works."
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Before you start coding, know your organization’s standards and procedures so your code conforms to style, format, and content expectations.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "During your career, you are likely to work on many different software projects, writing code in many application domains using a variety of tools and techniques."
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Thus, it is very important for others to be able to understand not only what you have written, but also why you have written it and how it fits in with their work."
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "For these reasons, you must know your organization's standards and procedures before you begin to write code."
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "When documenting code, include a block of comments at the opening that tells readers what the code does and gives an overview of the approach.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The opening section might look like this:\nThis block of comments tells the reader what the code does and gives an overview of the  approach."
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Organize, format, and document code to make it easy for others to understand what it does and how it works, since maintenance, testing, and integration teams may use your code later.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "it is essential that you organize, format, and document your code to make it easy for others to understand what it does and how it works."
