  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Here is a concrete way to satisfy statement testing and all-uses testing on a simple code component, using the concepts described in the provided material. For statement testing, ensure every statement is executed at least once. For all-uses testing, ensure the test set includes at least one path from every definition to every use that can be reached by that definition.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Statement testing: Every statement in the component is executed at least once in some test.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "All-uses testing: The test set includes at least one path from every definition to every use that can be reached by that definition.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Example alignment with the materials: To illustrate statement testing, design tests that execute statements 1 through 7 in the component (as described in the material’s Figure 8.7 example). For branch testing, identify the two decisions and create tests that traverse both yes/no branches. For path testing, create tests that cover every distinct path through the code. The related text states: \"In our example, statement testing requires fewer test cases than branch testing, which in turn requires fewer cases than path testing.\"", 
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In our example, statement testing requires fewer test cases than branch testing, which in turn requires fewer cases than path testing.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Stronger strategy and test-case requirements: In terms of fault detection, all-uses testing can be stronger than statement testing (Beizer-style comparisons show all-uses achieving higher fault coverage than some simpler strategies). Beizer’s discussion and related comparisons indicate that different strategies yield different fault-finding effectiveness, with all-uses testing reaching higher fault detection in the cited example. Additionally, the material notes that path testing generally requires more test cases than statement testing, indicating that a stronger strategy (path testing) typically incurs more test cases than a weaker one (statement testing).",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "All-uses testing: The test set includes at least one path from every definition to every use that can be reached by that definition.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In our example, statement testing requires fewer test cases than branch testing, which in turn requires fewer cases than path testing.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Ntafos (1984) compared random testing with branch testing and all-uses testing on seven mathematical programs with known faults. He found that random testing found 79.5% of the faults, branch testing found 85.5%, and all-uses testing found 90%.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "System testing has a different objective than unit and integration testing: whereas unit and integration testing aim to ensure the code implements the design, system testing aims to ensure that the system does what the customer wants it to do.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The objective of unit and integration testing was to ensure that the code implemented the design properly; that is, that the programmers wrote code to do what the designers intended.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In system testing, we have a very different objective: to ensure that the system does what the customer wants it to do.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "System testing focuses on verifying the system's overall functionality in alignment with requirements, as opposed to just validating individual components or their interfaces.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Once we are sure that information is passed among components in accordance with the design, we test the system to ensure that it has the desired functionality.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "System testing also emphasizes realism and the risk of failure in real-world use, going beyond isolated component checks.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Integration testing is useful to test combinations of components, but system testing adds more realism-and more chance for failure to occur.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Function testing addresses the functional requirements, while performance testing addresses the nonfunctional requirements.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Thus, functional testing addresses the functional requirements, and performance testing addresses the nonfunctional requirements.",
                "img_id": null
            }
            ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "The test team assigns primary responsibility for function and performance testing to the developers, with the customer playing a large role in acceptance and installation tests.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "the developers have primary responsibility for function and performance testing, but the customer plays a large role in acceptance and installation tests.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Acceptance testing enables customers and users to determine if the system meets their needs and expectations, with acceptance tests written, conducted, and evaluated by the customers (with developer assistance only when requested).",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The purpose of acceptance testing is to enable the customers and users to determine if the system we built really meets their needs and expectations.Thus, acceptance tests are written, conducted, and evaluated by the customers, with assistance from the developers only when the  customer  requests  an  answer  to  a technical  question.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Installation testing involves installing the system at user sites, focusing on completeness of the installed system and verification of any functional or nonfunctional characteristics affected by site conditions.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The final round of testing involves installing the system at user sites. If acceptance testing has been performed on-site, installation testing may not be needed. However, if the acceptance testing conditions were not the same as the actual site conditions, additional testing is necessary. To begin installation testing, we configure the system to the user environment.We attach the proper number and kind of devices to the main processor and establish communications with other systems.We allocate files and assign access to appropriate functions and data.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The tests focus on two things: completeness of the installed system and verification of any functional or nonfunctional characteristics that may be affected by site conditions.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

